!/docs/plans/
# .claude/: commit `skills/`, drop everything else (worktrees, local settings,
# session state).
#
# The leading negation is defensive, not redundant. Git prunes an excluded
# *directory* before reading any re-include inside it, so if a clone-local
# `.git/info/exclude` or a global core.excludesFile lists `.claude/`, the two
# lines below are never evaluated and `skills/` silently stays invisible --
# tracked here, absent there, with no error either way. Those files are
# per-clone and unshared, so this repo cannot know whether one applies; this
# line makes the outcome the same either way.
!/.claude/
/.claude/*
!/.claude/skills/
